 ("lucius_rom","luke","possible","Loukas is a short form of Lucius, a conjecture known to Origen. Against it: Lucius is Paul's 'kinsman', while Colossians 4:11–14 lists Luke apart from the fellow workers 'of the circumcision'."),
 ("sosthenes_acts","sosthenes_cor","possible","If the synagogue ruler beaten at Corinth later believed, he would be a natural co-sender of a letter to Corinth. Acts does not say he did."),
 ("erastus_acts","erastus_rom","possible","Both are tied to Corinth, but a sitting city treasurer is an unlikely travelling assistant. 2 Timothy 4:20 could belong to either."),
 ("gaius_mac","gaius_derbe","possible","A Western reading at Acts 20:4 makes Gaius a man of Doberus in Macedonia, which would merge the two; the usual text keeps them apart."),
 ("gaius_corinth","justus_corinth","possible","E. J. Goodspeed proposed one Corinthian householder named Gaius Titius Justus. It depends on the reading 'Titius Justus', which WEB does not print."),
